---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/back-in-stock';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/back-in-stock');
const desc = 'Plan back-in-stock SMS around exact variants, sellable inventory, waitlist permission, fair allocation, purchase exits, and incremental contribution.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700 hover:text-slate-950">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Back-in-stock alerts</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Send a restock alert only while the requested variant is buyable</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A restock waitlist records a specific request. Connect that request to current sellable inventory, then release messages in controlled waves. An alert is an invitation to check availability; it is not a reservation unless checkout actually enforces one.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Explore app reviews</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">Coordinate channels</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Define the inventory authority before collecting requests</h2>
      <p>Store the immutable product and variant identifiers, selected size or color, destination market, and fulfillment scope. A product-level restock must not wake a customer waiting for a different size. Preserve the variant link and verify its destination after catalog edits; do not silently substitute a replacement SKU.</p>
      <p>Shopify distinguishes available stock from incoming, committed, and unavailable quantities. On-hand inventory includes more than sellable stock. See <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/inventory-and-locations/fundamentals/inventory-states">Shopify's inventory-state definitions</a>. For this workflow, name the system that owns sellable quantity and document how locations eligible to fulfill this customer's order map to it. A warehouse receipt, stock at a pickup-only location, or an ERP feed timestamp alone should not authorize an online alert.</p>
      <p>Check publication, market availability, fulfillment eligibility, inventory freshness, and checkout policy as well as quantity. A setting that permits selling past zero does not establish that a restock arrived. If a bundle needs several components, resolve its actual sellable capacity. Pause alerts when the authority is unavailable or systems disagree; reconcile the discrepancy before releasing a queue.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Keep request identity, permission, and purpose together</h2>
      <p>Give each waitlist entry a request ID linked to the variant, customer identity where known, normalized destination, channel, capture time, disclosure version, and request state. Record what the person asked to receive, the permission evidence, and any expiry you disclosed. An email request is not evidence of SMS permission; a restock request is not an invitation to enroll the person in unrelated promotions.</p>
      <p>Deduplicate repeated submissions for the same identity, destination, variant, and active request. Do not merge different people merely because a household shares an address. Offer a way to withdraw a variant request without requiring a purchase. Apply channel opt-outs and current suppression at dispatch, and keep request withdrawal distinct from broader marketing preferences. Unknown identity or missing permission should hold the job for resolution.</p>
      <p>Set sending windows and message rules for the destination and program with appropriate advice; no single schedule here establishes compliance. Coordinate restock messages with other flows and preserve suppression evidence during migration. The <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">sending-window guide</a> provide related operating questions.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Allocate scarce units before opening a wave</h2>
      <p>Write the access policy before the restock: request-time order, a disclosed priority tier, or a documented lottery. Use a stable tie-breaker and retain the decision record. Do not quietly move high-spend customers ahead of a queue advertised as first come, first served. Explain whether priority governs notification order or an actual purchase reservation.</p>
      <p>Separate the count of units from the count of people notified. Set wave size from current sellable units, units allowed per order, competing storefront demand, and observed response uncertainty. A message-to-stock ratio is an operator choice, not a guarantee of availability. If reservations exist, verify atomic allocation, ownership, expiry, release, and checkout enforcement. Otherwise say clearly that stock may sell out.</p>
      <p>Before each wave and again when a deferred message becomes sendable, reread current stock and request eligibility. Stop the next wave when stock falls below the operating buffer or freshness limit. Recheck at landing and checkout too: a positive inventory check before dispatch cannot prevent concurrent buyers from consuming the same units. Avoid exact remaining-stock claims unless they can remain accurate when read.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Make exits and replay safe</h2>
      <p>Use explicit states such as waiting, allocated, queued, dispatched, purchased, withdrawn, expired, and paused. Define which transitions may reopen a request. A qualifying order for the requested variant exits its pending alerts across connected channels; an unrelated purchase does not necessarily satisfy that request. Decide how guest orders match the request without guessing. A cancellation or refund must not silently re-enroll someone who withdrew.</p>
      <p>Give inventory changes a recorded event time, ingestion time, version where available, and reconciliation status. Duplicate or late events should prompt a current-state read rather than replaying an old positive quantity. Tie a send idempotency key to the request, variant, and restock cycle, with one coordinator across email and SMS. A new restock cycle needs an explicit policy; repeated stock adjustments within the same cycle must not manufacture more alerts.</p>
      <p>At dispatch, reject jobs with an obsolete stock version, expired allocation, satisfied request, withdrawal, or missing permission. On an uncertain provider response, reconcile the provider message ID before retrying; a timeout does not prove the message failed. Keep a bounded retry policy, an operator pause switch, and a record of rejected jobs. A replay of the event log should rebuild state without sending messages.</p>
      <p>If overselling or stale alerts occur, stop queued waves, investigate the inventory and checkout race, and reconcile affected orders. Support should explain the actual order status and available refund or fulfillment options. Do not promise a replacement date without confirmed supply or move someone to a substitute waitlist without their choice. Resume only after stock, permissions, and dedupe state agree; corrections must not become another automatic promotional wave.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Choose a tool by the controls you can demonstrate</h2>
      <p>A dedicated restock integration is a useful fit when it can own variant requests and allocate scarce stock. An existing messaging platform is a useful fit when its integration can preserve those same controls. Ask for a demonstration using your catalog, locations, checkout settings, and suppression events before selecting either approach.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>If considering <a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> or <a class="link" href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a>, require the proposed SMS setup to show exact-variant capture, inventory authority, dispatch-time checks, and purchase exits.</li>
        <li>If considering <a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> or <a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> for shared channel ownership, demonstrate one coordinated request state so email and SMS do not independently consume the same allocation.</li>
        <li>If considering <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> for the email portion, establish how the proposed setup receives variant requests and shares withdrawal and purchase exits with the restock coordinator.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These are conditional evaluation paths, not claims that each supplier implements this design. Confirm current integration scope, support responsibility, message charges, contact charges, and recovery access directly with the vendor. Include reconciliation work and peak waitlist volume in the cost model.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Proposed acceptance fixtures for a restock pilot</h2>
      <p>The following scenarios are hypothetical proposed acceptance fixtures, not merchant results or promises about an app.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Wrong size or location:</strong> a medium shirt returns only at a location that cannot fulfill the customer's market. The small-size request and the ineligible medium request both remain waiting; neither receives an alert.</li>
        <li><strong>Receipt without availability:</strong> a shipment arrives in quality control. Incoming or unavailable units do not open a wave. Only a reconciled, currently sellable state can make the request eligible.</li>
        <li><strong>Repeated capture:</strong> one customer submits the same size request twice by SMS and once by email. Preserve each channel's permission evidence while the coordinator prevents duplicate allocation and applies the chosen contact policy.</li>
        <li><strong>Scarce wave:</strong> ten units become sellable while fifty requests wait. Apply the disclosed queue rule, allow for storefront demand, and check stock again before the next wave. No fixture assumes that all notified people can buy.</li>
        <li><strong>Purchase or withdrawal during delay:</strong> a queued alert waits for its sending window. A matched purchase or withdrawal arrives first. Dispatch rejects the job even if the audience export still contains the customer.</li>
        <li><strong>Late event and uncertain delivery:</strong> an old positive inventory event arrives after sellout and a provider timeout is retried. Current-state reconciliation blocks the stale wave; provider reconciliation prevents a second message for the same send key.</li>
        <li><strong>Recovery after oversell:</strong> checkout accepts more orders than can be fulfilled. Pause notifications, reconcile reservations and orders, give support an accurate affected-order list, and rebuild queue state without replaying dispatch.</li>
        <li><strong>Identity uncertainty:</strong> a guest order cannot be confidently matched to the waitlist. Hold the potentially stale request for resolution under the documented identity policy rather than merging unrelated profiles or claiming the purchase exit succeeded.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Measure contribution with comparable access to inventory</h2>
      <p>Define the eligible cohort before dispatch: same variant, market, permission rules, waitlist age band, and restock opportunity. Randomize within those strata and retain assignment for failed deliveries and non-buyers. Decide whether the test measures SMS added to email, an alert versus no alert, or a different wave policy; keep the other channel exposure consistent with that question.</p>
      <p>Scarce stock creates interference: treatment customers can exhaust units that control customers might otherwise buy. Record stock availability and access windows for both arms. Where individual assignment cannot provide comparable access, consider randomizing comparable restock cycles or variant groups with enough independent units to estimate uncertainty. Report unequal stock exposure as a limitation rather than calling every attributed order incremental.</p>
      <p>For each assigned arm, calculate net sales after discounts and refunds, less goods, variable fulfillment, payment, messaging, and support costs, divided by eligible assigned customers. Incremental contribution per eligible customer is the treatment value minus the control value. Deduct shared platform and setup costs separately when estimating rollout profit; do not subtract discounts twice if net sales already includes them.</p>
      <p>Use the same observation window, currency policy, and refund maturity for both arms. Track delivery failures, duplicate alerts, wait time, stockouts after clicks, opt-outs, complaints, cancellations, and support load alongside contribution. Prespecify the decision window and uncertainty threshold; a small sold-out restock may not support a reliable lift estimate. The <a class="link" href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics playbook</a> covers attribution and holdouts, and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> helps evaluate full operating cost.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
